// Each entry is authored: mission, concept, build task, interview prompt, answer rubric.
const lessons:[string,string,string,string,string][] = [
['The broken shopping cart','The + operator concatenates when one operand is a string. Explicit conversion and finite-number validation belong at the input boundary.','Implement calculateTotal(items) for price strings and integer quantities; reject invalid prices.','Why does "10" + 5 produce "105", and why is Number("") a validation trap?','Explain string concatenation, explicit conversion, Number("") returning zero, and rejecting blank or non-finite input before calculating.'],
['A counter with a secret','A closure retains access to its lexical environment after the outer call finishes. Different factory calls can have independent state.','Write createCounter() with increment and read methods; prove two counters are independent.','What does a closure retain, and when can it retain more memory than intended?','Describe lexical bindings rather than a frozen value; show independent environments and an event listener retaining a large referenced object.'],
['The object that changed twice','Objects are assigned by reference. A spread copy is shallow, so nested objects remain shared.','Update a nested application record without mutating the original; test the old and new values.','Why can {...record} still allow a nested mutation to affect record?','Explain shared nested references and copying each changed level; mention structuredClone limitations for functions.'],
['Race the event loop','Synchronous code completes before queued Promise callbacks. Timers become eligible after their delay; the delay is not an exact execution time.','Predict and run sync logs, Promise.resolve().then logs, and setTimeout logs in a browser.','In a simple browser script, why does a resolved Promise callback run before a zero-delay timer?','Describe the call stack, microtask checkpoint, and task queue. Avoid claiming every Node scheduling case has identical ordering.'],
['The request that never finishes','Await suspends an async function, not the whole JavaScript runtime. Failures need explicit handling and cleanup.','Write a fetch wrapper with response.ok checking, an AbortController timeout, and a finally cleanup.','Why does fetch need an HTTP status check even when it does not throw?','Distinguish network rejection from HTTP error responses; check status, preserve error context, and clear the timer in finally.'],
['Find the disappearing commit','Git commits are snapshots; branches are movable references. Small changes and reproducible steps make debugging easier.','Create a practice repository, introduce a bug, use git diff and git log to locate it, and revert the bad commit.','When would you use revert instead of reset on a shared branch?','Revert adds a history-preserving inverse commit; reset moves a reference and may discard changes depending on mode.'],
['Boss battle: repair the cart','Combine input validation, immutable updates, and asynchronous error handling without looking at yesterday’s solution.','Build a cart summary with loading, error, empty, and success states; record a five-minute explanation.','A discount request fails halfway through checkout. What state should the user see?','Keep the current cart intact, expose a recoverable error, prevent an invalid final price, and demonstrate tests rather than assuming success.'],
['Make illegal states harder to write','TypeScript narrows unions using checks. Static types do not validate untrusted JSON at runtime.','Model loading, success, and error as a discriminated union; render every state without any.','Why does casting an API response with as User not prove that it contains a valid user?','A type assertion changes compile-time interpretation only; validate unknown data before narrowing it.'],
['The render is a snapshot','Each React render sees its own state snapshot. Functional updates derive the next state from queued previous state.','Implement an add-three button using functional state updates and contrast it with three direct updates.','Why can three setCount(count + 1) calls result in one increment?','Each call reads the same render snapshot; updater functions compose through React’s update queue.'],
['Stop the endless effect','Effects synchronize with external systems. Dependencies reflect reactive values read by the effect, and cleanup undoes synchronization.','Repair a subscription that duplicates listeners; add cleanup and verify unmounting removes it.','Why is suppressing the dependency linter usually the wrong fix for a repeating effect?','Fix unstable dependencies or unnecessary effects; do not hide reactive reads. Explain setup/cleanup symmetry.'],
['The search result from the past','Network requests can complete in a different order from when they started. The UI must associate results with the active query.','Build a debounced search using controlled input and abort or ignore stale requests.','How would you prove that a slow earlier request cannot overwrite a newer result?','Use controlled promise timing in a test; resolve the newer request first, then verify the earlier result is ignored.'],
['Follow the server/client boundary','Server Components can read server-side data; Client Components enable state and browser interactions. Props crossing the boundary must be serializable.','Build a Next.js page with a server-rendered list and a small client-side filter; keep secrets on the server.','Where should an API secret live, and why is NEXT_PUBLIC_ unsuitable for it?','Keep secrets in server-only code. NEXT_PUBLIC_ values are exposed in browser bundles; a component boundary is not an authorization boundary.'],
['A form everyone can finish','Accessible forms connect labels, validation errors, focus, and keyboard behavior. A visible success state closes the feedback loop.','Build a job-application form with labels, field errors, keyboard navigation, and a submission state.','How do you communicate a validation error to both sighted users and screen-reader users?','Use a visible message tied with aria-describedby, mark invalid fields, and move focus appropriately after failed submission.'],
['Boss battle: fix the frontend','Debug state, effect cleanup, stale requests, and form behavior using evidence.','Build a searchable application list with controlled inputs and four UI states; test it without a mouse.','When would you measure before adding memoization?','Profile the slow interaction, locate the expensive render or computation, and verify a measurable improvement before retaining optimization.'],
['Read an HTTP conversation','HTTP separates method, target, headers, status, and body. APIs need a consistent contract that clients can understand.','Specify GET /applications and POST /applications with examples for success and invalid input.','How do 400, 401, 403, 404, and 409 differ in this API?','Map malformed input, missing identity, denied access, absent resource, and state conflict to distinct situations.'],
['Build the first Express endpoint','Middleware runs in registration order. A handler should validate, call domain logic, and send one response.','Implement a health endpoint and an in-memory applications endpoint with a documented response shape.','What happens if middleware neither sends a response nor calls next?','The request remains pending; explain termination or delegation and why a handler must not send twice.'],
['Guard the API boundary','A request body is untrusted data. Validation includes types, allowed values, lengths, and unexpected fields.','Validate company, role, stage, and application date; return field-specific errors.','Why is frontend validation insufficient even when every user uses your frontend?','Clients can bypass the UI, send stale or modified requests, and directly call endpoints; enforce invariants server-side.'],
['One error path, useful evidence','Centralized errors give clients stable messages and operators diagnostic context. Logs must not expose credentials.','Add error middleware with a request ID and map validation, missing record, and internal errors.','What should the client see when a database connection fails?','Return a generic actionable failure with correlation ID; log safe diagnostics server-side without leaking stack traces or secrets.'],
['Keep the event loop moving','CPU-heavy JavaScript can delay unrelated requests. Async syntax alone does not move computation off the event loop.','Compare a CPU-heavy route with a lightweight route; measure their latency under simultaneous requests.','Why does putting a long loop inside an async function still block other requests?','The loop executes synchronously until a yield; consider worker threads, a job queue, or reducing work.'],
['A list that stays navigable','Pagination needs a deterministic order. Offset pagination is simple, while cursors better handle some changing datasets.','Add bounded page size, stage filtering, and a stable createdAt/id ordering to the list API.','What can happen to offset pages when records are inserted between requests?','Records may repeat or be skipped as offsets shift; explain a stable cursor and its tradeoffs.'],
['Boss battle: defend your API','A working endpoint is more than the happy path. Contracts and failure tests make behavior dependable.','Implement create/list/detail endpoints with validation and tests for malformed JSON, empty results, and missing IDs.','What evidence would convince a reviewer that your API is ready for frontend integration?','Show a documented contract, representative requests, deterministic tests, stable errors, and known limitations.'],
['Model the application record','Document databases let related data live together, but access patterns should drive embedding and references.','Design application documents with ownerId, company, stage, timestamps, and bounded notes.','When should notes be embedded and when should they become separate documents?','Consider bounded size, independent access, growth, atomic updates, and MongoDB document size limits.'],
['Queries deserve indexes','Indexes accelerate matching access patterns but cost storage and write work. Compound key order affects usable query prefixes.','Create an ownerId/stage/createdAt index for the list query and inspect explain output.','Why might an index on stage alone be a poor fit for a per-user dashboard?','Low selectivity and missing owner filtering can scan unnecessary records; justify compound order using the actual query.'],
['Turn records into a funnel','Aggregation pipelines transform a set of documents through stages such as match, group, and sort.','Count applications per stage for one owner and produce a dashboard summary.','Why must the ownership filter apply to aggregation endpoints too?','Aggregations can leak other users’ data; scope the dataset before grouping and test with two owners.'],
['Identity is not permission','Authentication establishes identity. Authorization checks whether that identity can perform a specific action on a resource.','Protect application routes with authenticated identity and owner-scoped queries.','Why is a hard-to-guess record ID not access control?','IDs can leak or be shared; every resource operation needs an ownership or role check.'],
['Sessions without secret leaks','Session design includes storage, expiration, revocation, and transport. XSS and CSRF have different causes and defenses.','Write a session threat model and implement logout, expiration handling, and a protected route.','What does HttpOnly protect against, and what does it not prevent?','It prevents JavaScript reading the cookie, but not an XSS payload issuing authenticated actions; discuss CSRF and SameSite separately.'],
['Keep hostile input out','Security controls should match threats: parameterized queries, output encoding, bounded requests, and least privilege.','Test unexpected MongoDB operators, oversized input, and repeated login attempts in a local test environment.','Why should you avoid passing the raw request body directly into a database filter?','Attackers may influence operators and query shape; construct allowed fields explicitly and validate values.'],
['Boss battle: two users, zero leaks','Authorization must hold on create, list, detail, update, delete, and aggregate paths.','Create two test users and verify neither can access or modify the other’s application records.','How would you test authorization without relying on the UI hiding buttons?','Call endpoints directly with each identity and foreign IDs; assert denied writes and no data in read responses.'],
['Think in relationships','Primary keys identify rows, foreign keys relate them, and constraints preserve facts regardless of application code.','Create users, applications, and notes tables with foreign keys and sensible uniqueness constraints.','Why should a critical invariant be enforced in the database as well as the API?','Multiple writers and concurrent requests can bypass application assumptions; constraints provide a shared enforcement point.'],
['Join without losing the empty rows','An inner join keeps matches. A left join also keeps unmatched left rows; filtering the right side in WHERE can remove them.','List every application with its note count, including applications with no notes.','Why can a WHERE condition on the right table accidentally change a left join’s meaning?','Unmatched right columns are NULL; the predicate removes them. Move appropriate conditions into ON or handle NULL deliberately.'],
['Group the interview funnel','WHERE filters input rows; HAVING filters groups. Aggregates need clear handling of NULL and duplicate joined rows.','Report weekly application counts and stages with at least three applications.','How do COUNT(*), COUNT(column), and COUNT(DISTINCT column) differ?','Explain all rows, non-NULL values, and distinct non-NULL values; give an example with a missing value.'],
['All or nothing','Transactions group changes into one commit or rollback. Isolation determines what concurrent transactions can observe.','Create an application and its first audit note in one transaction; intentionally fail the second insert.','Does using a transaction automatically prevent every race condition?','No; isolation level, constraints, locking, and transaction logic matter. Atomicity alone does not eliminate lost updates.'],
['Read the query plan','EXPLAIN describes a plan; EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the statement and reports runtime observations.','Measure a SELECT on a realistic local dataset before and after an index; compare scan and row estimates.','Why should you be careful running EXPLAIN ANALYZE on a write statement?','It actually executes the statement; use a safe environment and transaction rollback when appropriate.'],
['Your progress belongs to you','Supabase uses PostgreSQL with authentication and row-level security. A browser publishable key relies on RLS for data isolation.','Create a progress table keyed by user_id and policies that restrict select/insert/update/delete to auth.uid().','Why must a service-role key never be shipped to the browser?','It bypasses RLS; a publishable key plus an authenticated session is the browser pattern, with tested policies.'],
['Boss battle: query clinic','Use SQL to answer product questions and prove data isolation.','Write five queries: stage totals, recent applications, zero-note applications, weekly totals, and latest note per application.','How would you investigate a dashboard count that doubles after adding a join?','Inspect join cardinality, reproduce with multiple child rows, and use pre-aggregation or appropriate distinct counting.'],
['Connect the real user journey','Integration exposes contract mismatches that isolated components hide. Model loading and failure as first-class states.','Connect the tracker form to the API and refresh the list after a confirmed save.','When is optimistic UI appropriate, and what must happen on failure?','Use it when rollback and conflict behavior are clear; restore or reconcile state and explain pending versus confirmed changes.'],
['Filters worth bookmarking','URL parameters can encode shareable UI state. Parse and validate them before querying.','Persist stage, search term, and page in the URL; restore them on reload and browser back.','Which state belongs in the URL rather than only in component state?','Shareable navigation/filter state is a good fit; secrets, temporary text drafts, and sensitive data generally are not.'],
['Files need boundaries','Uploads need limits, type checks, ownership, and a storage policy. Filenames supplied by users are not trusted paths.','Design a private resume upload flow with size limits and expiring download access.','Why is checking only a filename extension insufficient?','Names can lie; validate content/type as appropriate, restrict storage and access, and avoid serving active content unsafely.'],
['Test behavior, not implementation','Useful tests observe outcomes and important boundaries instead of duplicating private implementation details.','Write tests for form validation, owner-scoped API access, and a failed save preserving input.','Why can a test that mocks every internal function give misleading confidence?','It may verify wiring while missing broken integration; keep key contracts real and mock external boundaries deliberately.'],
['Complete the keyboard journey','Accessibility testing includes focus order, names, errors, status messages, and usable layouts at zoom.','Complete the tracker journey using only a keyboard and at 200% zoom; fix the first three barriers.','How should focus behave when a modal opens and closes?','Move focus inside, keep it within a modal, support Escape where appropriate, and restore focus to the trigger.'],
['Measure the slow screen','Performance work starts with a reproducible user action and a measured bottleneck. Network, render, and data costs differ.','Profile a large application list and reduce one measured bottleneck; record before and after evidence.','Why might useMemo fail to improve an application with a slow API?','It targets repeated computation during rendering, not network/database latency; optimize the measured bottleneck.'],
['Boss battle: release candidate','A release needs a reproducible setup, tested flows, and honest limitations.','Run registration-to-application creation, filter, edit, and logout; write a release checklist and demo script.','What would you include in a README so another developer can run your project?','Setup, environment variables without secrets, database steps, scripts, architecture, tests, and limitations.'],
['Python through familiar ideas','Python lists, dictionaries, sets, and tuples map to familiar collection concepts but have different APIs and mutability.','Transform application dictionaries into counts by stage using a loop and then a comprehension.','When is a set more appropriate than a list in Python?','Use membership/deduplication when ordering or duplicates are not needed; explain hashability and average lookup cost.'],
['The mutable default trap','Python evaluates default arguments once when defining a function. A mutable default can be shared across calls.','Repair add_note(note, notes=[]) and prove separate calls no longer share a list.','Why is using None as a default helpful here?','Create the list inside the function when the argument is None; show that the original default persisted between calls.'],
['Errors with a useful boundary','Catch exceptions where you can recover or add context. Context managers guarantee cleanup around resources.','Read a CSV with a context manager and report file errors separately from invalid rows.','Why should you avoid wrapping an entire program in except Exception: pass?','It hides failures, corrupts assumptions, and makes debugging difficult; catch specific exceptions and preserve context.'],
['From function to FastAPI route','Type hints describe expected types; FastAPI and its validation models enforce request shapes at runtime.','Create GET /health and POST /imports/preview with validated request and response models.','How does a plain Python type hint differ from runtime request validation?','Python generally does not enforce annotations itself; a framework/model layer parses and validates data.'],
['Parse the messy spreadsheet','CSV parsing must handle quoting, missing fields, blank lines, and invalid records. A preview should not silently import bad rows.','Parse a CSV using the standard csv module and return accepted rows plus row-numbered errors.','Why is splitting every line on commas not a correct general CSV parser?','Quoted fields can contain commas and newlines, and quote escaping has rules; use the CSV parser.'],
['Test the importer contract','A deterministic test fixture makes parser behavior reproducible. Boundary cases matter more than many happy-path examples.','Test blank input, quoted commas, duplicate rows, missing columns, and a valid import preview.','Should one invalid row fail the whole import? Defend an explicit policy.','Either policy can work if documented; explain atomic import versus partial acceptance and a clear error report.'],
['Boss battle: Python mini-service','Transfer your HTTP, validation, and testing knowledge to Python.','Deliver a FastAPI CSV preview service with documented examples and a command that runs its tests.','What Python-specific mistake did you encounter, and how did a test expose it?','Use a concrete example such as mutable defaults, parsing, exception scope, or blocking I/O; show the failed and fixed behavior.'],
['Persist the import safely','Database sessions and transactions need clear lifetimes. Close resources and roll back failed work.','Persist validated applications into PostgreSQL in one transaction using a scoped database session.','What should happen to the session after a database error during a transaction?','Roll back before reuse or close it; explain why continuing a failed transaction is invalid.'],
['Two clicks, one import','Idempotency lets a retry represent the same operation. A database uniqueness guarantee handles concurrent duplicates.','Add an owner-scoped import idempotency key and return the previous result for an identical retry.','Why is checking for a key and then inserting without a constraint unsafe?','Concurrent requests can both pass the check; use an atomic uniqueness guarantee and handle the conflict.'],
['The network beneath fetch','DNS resolves names; TCP establishes a reliable stream; TLS authenticates and encrypts; HTTP defines application messages.','Draw the stages of loading your API and list a failure symptom for each stage.','How would you distinguish a DNS failure from an HTTP 500?','DNS failure occurs before an HTTP response; a 500 means an HTTP server responded. Inspect timing, resolution, and response evidence.'],
['Threads, tasks, and blocking work','Concurrency overlaps work; parallelism executes work simultaneously. Async I/O helps waiting workloads but does not make CPU work free.','Compare a blocking call inside an async route with a correctly isolated or asynchronous alternative.','Why might an async endpoint become slower under concurrent requests?','Blocking code may monopolize the event loop; inspect CPU, synchronous I/O, connection pools, and downstream limits.'],
['Keep updates from disappearing','Two readers may write conflicting changes. Optimistic concurrency checks a version; locking coordinates access differently.','Add a version field to application updates and return a conflict when an old version is submitted.','What should a UI do when the server rejects a stale update?','Preserve the user’s draft, fetch current state, and let the user reconcile; avoid silently overwriting newer data.'],
['Retry without a stampede','Timeouts bound waiting. Retries need limits, backoff, jitter, and a safe repeatable operation.','Write a bounded retry wrapper for a read request and simulate temporary versus permanent failures.','Why should a client not retry every failed POST automatically?','It may duplicate side effects; use idempotency where supported and distinguish transient failures from validation/auth errors.'],
['Boss battle: failure lab','Reliable services make partial failures understandable and recoverable.','Simulate a database outage, duplicate import, and stale update; show what the user sees and how data remains consistent.','How do you distinguish a successful retry from a duplicated operation?','Use an operation key and persisted result, assert one side effect, and correlate retries in logs.'],
['Find your way around Linux','Processes, ports, permissions, environment variables, and signals form the operating environment of your application.','Practice listing processes, inspecting a listening port, reading logs, and gracefully stopping a local service.','Why is killing a process immediately different from giving it a termination signal?','Graceful termination permits draining and cleanup; forced termination cannot be handled by the process.'],
['Package the application','A container image contains the application and dependencies; a running container adds process state and a writable layer.','Write a Dockerfile with a locked dependency install and run the app as a non-root user.','Why copy dependency manifests before frequently changing source files?','Docker can reuse a dependency-install layer when manifests do not change; explain layer caching.'],
['Connect the containers','Containers communicate over networks using service names. localhost refers to the current container itself.','Use Compose for the API and local PostgreSQL with a named database volume and health check.','Why does localhost:5432 fail from the API container when PostgreSQL runs in another container?','The loopback points at the API container; use the database service hostname and internal port.'],
['A clean build every time','CI should reproduce installation, type checking, tests, and build from a clean checkout.','Create a GitHub Actions workflow for pull requests with a locked install and failing checks that block success.','Why is npm ci a better fit than an unpinned dependency update during CI?','It uses the lockfile and fails on manifest mismatch; reproducibility matters more than silently selecting new versions.'],
['Keep credentials out of artifacts','Build-time and runtime configuration have different lifetimes. Browser-exposed values are never secrets.','Inventory environment variables and classify public configuration, runtime secrets, and local-only values.','How could a secret accidentally appear in a frontend bundle even if .env is ignored by Git?','Build tooling can inline exposed variables; Git ignore only affects tracking, not bundled output.'],
['Deploy one artifact deliberately','Promote a known artifact, verify health, and retain a previous version for rollback. Tests are necessary but not a deployment health signal.','Design a pipeline with test, build, deploy, health check, and rollback steps; rehearse locally.','Why build once and promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding separately for each environment?','It reduces untracked differences; externalize environment-specific runtime configuration where possible.'],
['Boss battle: broken pipeline','A failed pipeline is evidence. Identify the failing stage and reproduce it in the same environment.','Introduce and repair a missing environment variable, failing test, and incompatible dependency lockfile.','What do you check first when code passes locally but fails in CI?','Compare runtime versions, lockfiles, environment, case-sensitive paths, timezone, and test isolation based on the actual error.'],
['Map the AWS boundaries','IAM controls actions on resources. Network reachability and application authorization are separate controls.','Draw a small AWS architecture and write a least-privilege policy proposal before creating resources.','Why is an open security group not equivalent to an IAM permission?','Security groups filter network traffic; IAM authorizes AWS API actions. Neither replaces application-level authorization.'],
['Launch with a rollback plan','Compute deployment needs a supported runtime, process management, health checks, and explicit network exposure.','Prepare an EC2 deployment runbook with startup, health verification, restart, rollback, and teardown commands.','What is the difference between the process running and the application being healthy?','A process may be alive while dependencies or routes fail; define readiness and representative health checks.'],
['Store objects privately','S3 stores objects by key. Access policy, encryption, lifecycle, and expiration should match the data.','Design private resume storage with short-lived signed access and a deletion policy.','Why should private resumes not be placed in a publicly readable bucket?','They contain personal information; restrict ownership and use controlled access, with expiry and deletion behavior.'],
['See the failure before guessing','Logs describe events, metrics summarize behavior, and traces connect work across boundaries. Correlation IDs tie evidence together.','Define latency, error-rate, and availability signals; write a troubleshooting flow using structured logs.','Why is average latency alone insufficient for understanding a slow user experience?','It hides tail latency and distributions; inspect percentiles, sample size, route breakdown, and error rates.'],
['Short-lived deployment identity','OIDC can exchange trusted workflow identity for temporary cloud credentials. Restrict which repository, branch, or environment is trusted.','Draft a GitHub-to-AWS OIDC role trust policy scoped to your repository and deployment environment.','Why is trusting every branch and repository with a wildcard subject dangerous?','Unintended workloads could assume the role; restrict issuer/audience/subject and apply environment protections.'],
['Spend deliberately, clean up fully','Cloud costs depend on running resources, storage, traffic, and retained artifacts. Budget alerts do not guarantee a hard spending cap.','Create a resource inventory, estimate your experiment cost, and rehearse teardown without provisioning paid resources yet.','What resources might remain billable after stopping a compute instance?','Depending on setup, volumes, snapshots, addresses, load balancers, and other services may remain; inspect the inventory and billing.'],
['Boss battle: deployment incident','Operational readiness means detecting, diagnosing, recovering, and documenting a failure.','Run a deployment exercise within an agreed budget or simulate locally; break configuration, inspect logs, roll back, and clean up.','How do you write a useful incident summary without blaming a person?','Describe impact, timeline, contributing conditions, recovery, and concrete preventive actions with owners.'],
['Ask before drawing boxes','System design starts with users, core actions, scale, latency, availability, consistency, and boundaries.','Clarify a URL-shortener brief and write functional and nonfunctional requirements plus explicit exclusions.','Which three questions would most change your initial URL-shortener architecture?','Ask about request volume/read-write ratio, retention/custom aliases, and availability/latency or abuse requirements.'],
['Estimate the shape of the load','Approximate requests, storage, and bandwidth with stated assumptions. Estimates guide decisions rather than pretending to be exact.','Estimate reads per second and yearly storage for a shortener with one million new links per day.','How would a peak-to-average traffic ratio influence capacity planning?','Calculate average first, state a peak factor, include headroom, and explain that observed traffic should refine the estimate.'],
['Design the short link','A shortener needs key generation, collision handling, redirects, storage, and abuse controls. Choose tradeoffs explicitly.','Draw the write and redirect paths with a data model and collision strategy.','What changes when you use random short codes rather than an encoded sequential ID?','Discuss enumeration, collision probability/checks, key space, predictability, and generation coordination.'],
['A cache can be wrong','Caches trade freshness and complexity for lower latency or load. Define keys, TTL, invalidation, and failure behavior.','Add a cache-aside design to the shortener and trace a miss, hit, expired value, and origin outage.','What causes a cache stampede and how might you reduce it?','Many clients rebuild the same expired value; consider request coalescing, jittered expiration, or serving stale data when acceptable.'],
['Queues move the waiting','A queue decouples acceptance from processing. Delivery and retries require consumer idempotency and an error policy.','Design application-reminder notifications with a queue, worker, retry limit, and dead-letter handling.','Why does at-least-once delivery require duplicate-safe consumers?','A message may be redelivered after ambiguous acknowledgment; deduplicate or make the side effect idempotent.'],
['Choose consistency consciously','Replication, partitioning, and load balancing solve different problems. Consistency requirements should follow the operation.','Scale the tracker to many users and compare replica reads with primary reads for freshly saved applications.','When might read-your-writes matter more than reading from the nearest replica?','Immediately after an update, users expect their change; discuss routing, session guarantees, and replication lag.'],
['Boss battle: design under pressure','A design interview tests requirements, data flow, choices, and response to new constraints.','Spend 40 minutes designing a notification service; introduce a worker outage halfway through and revise the design.','What would fail first at ten times the traffic, and what evidence would you collect?','Identify a plausible bottleneck tied to estimates, propose measurement, and avoid scaling every component without evidence.'],
['Tell the story of your work','An effective project explanation connects a real problem, your decisions, observed results, and lessons. Do not invent impact metrics.','Prepare a two-minute tracker walkthrough and a deeper explanation of one technical tradeoff.','Which part did you personally implement, and what would you change now?','Be specific about ownership, constraints, evidence, and a reasoned improvement; distinguish your work from library behavior.'],
['Frontend interview simulation','Clarify interaction requirements before coding. Show loading, errors, keyboard behavior, and cleanup.','Build a searchable list in 45 minutes, then spend 15 minutes testing and explaining it.','How would you adapt the list for thousands of results and a slow network?','Discuss server pagination, stale-request handling, measured rendering cost, and accessible feedback.'],
['Backend interview simulation','Implement a narrow correct contract before broadening features. State assumptions about identity and persistence.','Build a validated create/list API in 45 minutes and demonstrate invalid input and unauthorized access tests.','What would you change before allowing multiple instances of this service?','Remove process-local authoritative state, coordinate consistency in durable storage, and consider rate limits, sessions, and background jobs.'],
['Coding interview simulation','Think aloud: clarify, give a baseline, identify a pattern, implement, and test. Correctness comes before cleverness.','Solve one unseen easy and one unseen medium problem in 60 minutes without solutions.','How do you recover when you cannot find the optimal solution immediately?','Present a correct baseline, explain its complexity, inspect constraints, and improve a specific bottleneck with the interviewer.'],
['Behavioral answers with evidence','STAR structures a situation, task, action, and result. Honest detail is more credible than a rehearsed slogan.','Write stories about a bug, disagreement, deadline, mistake, and learning challenge using real experience.','Tell me about a technical disagreement and how you resolved it.','Explain competing goals, how you listened, evidence used, your action, the outcome, and what you learned.'],
['A resume that survives follow-ups','Resume claims should be relevant, specific, and defensible. Two years of experience is enough to discuss meaningful work without exaggeration.','Rewrite three bullets as action plus technical detail plus observed outcome; link to a clean project README.','Can you explain and demonstrate every technology and result listed on your resume?','Remove unsupported claims, distinguish familiarity from production experience, and prepare one concrete example per important skill.'],
['Boss battle: the full loop','Combine coding, project discussion, design, and behavioral communication. Score each separately to locate the real bottleneck.','Run a 150-minute mock loop with breaks and record a rubric score plus three targeted improvements.','What evidence shows your weakest area improved compared with your first assessment?','Compare distinct tasks, independent attempts, tests, explanations, and repeated performance rather than time spent studying.'],
['Repair the weakest link','Target the cause of an error: a missing concept, implementation habit, communication gap, or time management.','Choose your two lowest-scoring skills and solve new tasks that exercise the same underlying concepts.','Why is retyping a memorized solution weaker evidence than solving a new variation?','Recall of surface details can hide a missing mental model; transfer shows the principle can be applied independently.'],
['Make the project reviewable','A reviewer needs a working demo path, reproducible setup, clear architecture, and known limitations.','Audit your README, screenshots, environment example, tests, and deployment instructions from a fresh checkout.','What should a reviewer be able to verify in the first five minutes?','The purpose, your contribution, how to run it, one complete journey, and honest limitations.'],
['Apply with intent','Match your demonstrated skills to actual role requirements. A consistent application record supports thoughtful follow-up.','Shortlist ten roles, note requirements and gaps, and prepare tailored applications for the best matches.','How do you explain a missing technology without overstating your experience?','Connect transferable concepts, show relevant learning evidence, and state clearly what you have and have not used.'],
['Final readiness check','Independent performance across different days is stronger evidence than one good session. Assess against the target role.','Complete coding, protected API, project explanation, and a 40-minute design task; score with the saved rubric.','Which responsibilities can you take on confidently today, and where would you ask for guidance?','Use concrete evidence and clear limits; explain how you validate unfamiliar work and communicate risk.'],
['Practice the follow-up','Interview feedback becomes useful when converted into a small, testable learning action.','Simulate an interviewer changing requirements mid-solution; revise your approach and explain the tradeoff.','Your original solution is correct but too slow. How do you discuss the next improvement?','Acknowledge the constraint, quantify the bottleneck, propose an alternative, and test correctness after the change.'],
['Your next chapter starts here','A sustainable routine protects useful skills without endless cramming. Keep applying, revisiting weak concepts, and building.','Export your progress, write a four-week maintenance plan, and record a final project walkthrough.','What will you practice weekly after day 90, and how will you know it is working?','Choose targeted retrieval, an unseen problem, project maintenance, and interview feedback with observable checkpoints.']
];

type Pattern = {name:string;hint:string;rubric:string;problems:string[]};
const patterns:Pattern[] = [
{name:'Arrays & hash maps',hint:'Write a brute-force scan first. Ask what fact a Set or Map could remember so you do not scan again.',rubric:'State the map/set invariant, handle duplicates and empty input, and justify average O(n) time plus O(n) auxiliary space where applicable.',problems:['Two Sum: return indices of two distinct items summing to target; [2,7,11,15], 9 → [0,1].','Contains Duplicate: [1,2,3,1] → true; [] → false.','Valid Anagram: "listen", "silent" → true; compare character frequencies.','First Unique Character: "leetcode" → index 0; return -1 when none exists.','Intersection of Two Arrays: [1,2,2,3], [2,3,4] → [2,3] with unique output.','Group Anagrams: ["eat","tea","tan","ate"] → groups by letter counts.','Longest Consecutive Sequence: [100,4,200,1,3,2] → length 4.']},
{name:'Two pointers & sliding windows',hint:'Define what your current window or pair represents. Move one boundary only when you can explain which candidates are excluded.',rubric:'Describe the pointer/window invariant, bound each pointer movement, and test duplicates, an empty input, and a minimal valid window.',problems:['Valid Palindrome: ignore non-alphanumerics and case; "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" → true.','Two Sum in a Sorted Array: [1,3,4,6], target 7 → zero-based indices [0,3] or [1,2].','Move Zeroes: [0,1,0,3,12] → [1,3,12,0,0], preserving nonzero order.','Maximum Sum of a Size-k Window: [2,1,5,1,3,2], k=3 → 9.','Longest Substring Without Repeats: "abcabcbb" → length 3.','Minimum Positive-Number Subarray: [2,3,1,2,4,3], target 7 → length 2.','Longest Repeating Character Replacement: "AABABBA", k=1 → length 4.']},
{name:'Stacks, queues & linked lists',hint:'Draw the data structure after each operation. Preserve the reference you will need before overwriting a pointer.',rubric:'Explain the invariant and time per operation. Test empty/single-element structures and avoid losing links or using costly repeated array shifts without discussion.',problems:['Valid Parentheses: "([])" → true; "([)]" → false.','Min Stack: implement push, pop, top, and getMin in O(1) time per operation.','Queue with Two Stacks: demonstrate FIFO order for enqueue 1, enqueue 2, dequeue.','Reverse a Linked List: 1→2→3 becomes 3→2→1.','Merge Two Sorted Lists: 1→3 and 2→4 become 1→2→3→4.','Detect a Linked-list Cycle: return true if following next repeats a node.','Remove Nth Node from End: 1→2→3→4→5, n=2 removes 4; assume valid n.']},
{name:'Binary search & recursion',hint:'Write the base case or the meaning of low/high before the loop. Ensure the remaining search space strictly shrinks.',rubric:'Prove termination, preserve the search invariant, test boundary positions, and distinguish O(log n) searching from preprocessing or slicing costs.',problems:['Binary Search: sorted [-1,0,3,5,9,12], target 9 → index 4; absent target → -1.','Search Insert Position: [1,3,5,6], target 2 → 1.','First and Last Position: [5,7,7,8,8,10], target 8 → [3,4].','Search Rotated Sorted Array: [4,5,6,7,0,1,2], target 0 → 4; unique elements.','Find Minimum in Rotated Array: [3,4,5,1,2] → 1; unique elements.','Recursive Power: implement x^n for integer n, including negative n and n=0.','Koko Eating Bananas: [3,6,7,11], h=8 → minimum integer speed 4.']},
{name:'Trees & traversal',hint:'Decide what one recursive call returns. For level order, keep nodes grouped by depth using a queue.',rubric:'Handle null roots and skewed trees. Explain O(n) traversal work and O(h) recursion space or O(width) queue space as relevant.',problems:['Maximum Binary-tree Depth: a root with two leaf children has depth 2; empty tree has depth 0.','Invert a Binary Tree: swap left and right children at every node.','Same Tree: compare structure and values, not traversal values alone.','Level-order Traversal: root 3, children 9 and 20, grandchildren 15 and 7 under 20 → [[3],[9,20],[15,7]].','Validate a BST: every node must satisfy bounds inherited from all ancestors; use strict ordering.','Lowest Common Ancestor in a BST: return the split node for two existing target values.','Diameter of a Binary Tree: return the maximum number of edges between any two nodes.']},
{name:'Heaps & intervals',hint:'For intervals, sort by a meaningful endpoint. For top-k, decide whether the smallest retained item should be easy to remove.',rubric:'Include sorting/build costs in complexity, define overlap at endpoints, and test nested intervals or repeated values.',problems:['Merge Intervals: [[1,3],[2,6],[8,10]] → [[1,6],[8,10]], treating touching endpoints as overlapping.','Insert Interval: [[1,3],[6,9]] plus [2,5] → [[1,5],[6,9]].','Meeting Rooms: [[0,30],[5,10],[15,20]] → cannot attend all; adjacent endpoints do not conflict.','Minimum Meeting Rooms: [[0,30],[5,10],[15,20]] → 2.','Kth Largest Element: [3,2,1,5,6,4], k=2 → 5, counting duplicates.','Top K Frequent Elements: [1,1,1,2,2,3], k=2 → [1,2] in either order.','Merge K Sorted Lists: merge any number of sorted linked lists into one sorted list.']},
{name:'Backtracking',hint:'Describe a partial solution, available choices, and when to stop. Undo changes after exploring each choice.',rubric:'Explain pruning, avoid shared mutable-result bugs, and acknowledge output-dependent or exponential runtime.',problems:['Subsets: [1,2] → [[],[1],[2],[1,2]] in any order.','Permutations: [1,2,3] → all six orderings.','Combination Sum: candidates [2,3,6,7], target 7 → [[2,2,3],[7]], reuse allowed.','Generate Parentheses: n=3 → all five valid strings with three pairs.','Letter Combinations: digits "23" → ["ad","ae","af","bd","be","bf","cd","ce","cf"].','Word Search: find a word in a grid by horizontal/vertical moves without reusing a cell.','Palindrome Partitioning: "aab" → [["a","a","b"],["aa","b"]].']},
{name:'Graphs',hint:'Define nodes and edges, track visited state, and choose BFS for unweighted shortest paths or DFS for exploration.',rubric:'State adjacency representation, handle disconnected components and cycles, and justify O(V+E) traversal where applicable.',problems:['Number of Islands: count connected groups of 1s in a grid using four-directional neighbors.','Flood Fill: recolor the starting cell’s four-directionally connected same-color region.','Clone Graph: copy nodes and edges without retaining references to original nodes.','Rotting Oranges: each minute rot adjacent fresh oranges; return time or -1 if unreachable.','Course Schedule: detect whether all courses can finish given prerequisite edges.','Unweighted Shortest Path: return distance from source to target in an adjacency list, or -1.','Connected Components: count components in an undirected graph including isolated nodes.']},
{name:'Dynamic programming',hint:'State what dp[i] means in a full sentence. Derive its transition from smaller solved states and write base cases.',rubric:'Explain recurrence, initialization, evaluation order, and time/space costs. Compare against the repeated work in a recursive baseline.',problems:['Climbing Stairs: one or two steps at a time; n=4 → 5 ways; define n=0 as 1.','House Robber: [2,7,9,3,1] → 12 without adjacent selections.','Min Cost Climbing Stairs: [10,15,20] → 15; start at step 0 or 1.','Coin Change: [1,2,5], amount 11 → minimum 3 coins; impossible → -1.','Longest Increasing Subsequence: [10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18] → length 4; start with O(n²).','Unique Paths: a 3×7 grid with only right/down moves has 28 paths.','Longest Common Subsequence: "abcde", "ace" → length 3.']},
{name:'Mixed interview practice',hint:'Do not guess the pattern from the title. Use constraints, a baseline, and a counterexample to choose your approach.',rubric:'Clarify the contract, show a correct baseline and an improved approach, test edge cases, and explain complexity without hints.',problems:['Product of Array Except Self: [1,2,3,4] → [24,12,8,6], without division.','Daily Temperatures: [73,74,75,71,69,72,76,73] → [1,1,4,2,1,1,0,0].','Container With Most Water: [1,8,6,2,5,4,8,3,7] → maximum area 49.','Subarray Sum Equals K: [1,1,1], k=2 → count 2; negatives are allowed.','Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation: ["2","1","+","3","*"] → 9; division truncates toward zero.','Find All Anagrams: s="cbaebabacd", p="abc" → indices [0,6].','Jump Game: [2,3,1,1,4] → true; [3,2,1,0,4] → false.']},
{name:'Design-oriented algorithms',hint:'Separate the interface from internal state. Trace a sequence of operations, not only one call.',rubric:'Prove operation behavior and complexity, including eviction, duplicate keys, and empty-state behavior where relevant.',problems:['LRU Cache: implement get/put with expected O(1) operations and a fixed positive capacity.','Trie: implement insert, search, and startsWith for lowercase words.','Time-based Key-value Store: set(key,value,timestamp), get latest value at or before a query time; writes per key are ordered.','Kth Largest in a Stream: maintain the kth largest after each insertion, counting duplicates.','Median from Data Stream: maintain median after each added integer.','Task Scheduler: unit tasks with cooldown n; [A,A,A,B,B,B], n=2 → 8 intervals.','Design a Browser History: visit, back, forward; visiting after back clears forward history.']},
{name:'Timed assessment',hint:'Work independently first. If you need a hint, record it and retry a related variation on another day.',rubric:'Score correctness, edge cases, complexity, and explanation from 0–2 each; record evidence, not only a total.',problems:['Retry Two Sum using a new input with negative numbers and duplicate values.','Solve Longest Substring Without Repeats and return the substring as well as its length.','Merge Intervals and explain whether the input is mutated.','Solve Number of Islands without modifying the input grid.','Implement LRU Cache and test an update of an existing key at capacity.','Solve Coin Change and also return one optimal coin combination.','Mock pair: Valid Parentheses followed by Course Schedule; total time 60 minutes.']},
{name:'Transfer & retention',hint:'Choose unfamiliar inputs and write expected outputs before coding. Explain why the approach still holds.',rubric:'Demonstrate independent transfer and a clear invariant. Record any assistance and schedule the weak concept for another retrieval.',problems:['Implement binary search for the first value greater than or equal to a target, including duplicates.','Extend Group Anagrams to ignore case; explicitly define the allowed character set.','Return one actual shortest path in an unweighted graph rather than only its length.','Solve House Robber on a circular street where the first and last houses are adjacent.','Find the kth smallest BST value with an iterative traversal.','Final pair: unseen variation of sliding window plus a tree traversal; compare with your first-week evidence.']}
];
export type Quest = {day:number;chapter:number;title:string;lesson:string;mission:string;questions:{id:string;prompt:string;hint:string;rubric:string}[];steps:{id:string;title:string;detail:string;minutes:number}[];boss:boolean;pattern:string};
export const quests:Quest[]=lessons.map(([title,lesson,mission,question,rubric],i)=>{
 const chapter=Math.floor(i/7)+1, p=patterns[chapter-1], problem=p.problems[i%7], boss=(i+1)%7===0||i===89;
 return {day:i+1,chapter,title,lesson,mission,boss,pattern:p.name,
 questions:[{id:'concept',prompt:question,hint:lesson,rubric},{id:'dsa',prompt:problem,hint:p.hint,rubric:p.rubric},{id:'reflection',prompt:`Show your work for “${title}”: paste the implementation or a design outline, list three test cases with expected results, and explain one decision you would defend in an interview.`,hint:'Start with a normal case, a boundary case, and a failure case. State any assumptions.',rubric:`The evidence must address this mission: ${mission} Check correctness, boundary behavior, and an explicit tradeoff. Do not award implementation credit for a plan alone.`}],
 steps:[{id:'recall',title:'Warm up your memory',detail:i===0?'Without notes, explain a closure, an HTTP request, and a database index. Mark each clear, uncertain, or new.':'Open the Recall room. Try due questions without hints, then compare with the rubric and rate your recall.',minutes:15},{id:'investigate',title:'Investigate the mystery',detail:`Read the mission: ${mission} Write a predicted result and one way it could fail before you run anything.`,minutes:20},{id:'learn',title:'Build the mental model',detail:`Read the concept below and the chapter reference. Explain it in your own words, then answer: ${question}`,minutes:25},{id:'dsa',title:`Solve: ${p.name}`,detail:`${problem} Write a baseline, improve it if possible, and state time and space complexity.`,minutes:45},{id:'build',title:boss?'Complete the boss challenge':'Ship one working piece',detail:`${mission} Save your code or design evidence in the reflection answer. Include normal, boundary, and failure checks.`,minutes:55},{id:'explain',title:'Defend it in an interview',detail:'Answer all three questions. Use Review with ChatGPT for feedback after your attempt, then save the feedback and any correction.',minutes:15},{id:'reflect',title:'Leave a trail for future you',detail:'Record one mistake and its cause in your notes. Mark the steps you actually completed; schedule retrieval when the quest is complete.',minutes:5}]
 };
});
export const curriculumVersion='2026-09-14.1';
